al form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -8)(\\lambda -8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=8.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(8)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac392660-8c5c-5e68-838e-b85d4459418a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417021 #edb/ref 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-10-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-2)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-6)=[\\begin{aligned